      "textEn": "284\n\nWithout an officer, a learned person, Krishna cannot be understood. He who remains in the state of non-seeking, does not attain liberation.\n\nNeither by abandoning the senses nor by keeping them. Not by chanting, nor by water, nor by fire,\nWithout great adoration and anointing. 12. Meaning of the word\n\nRahugan—Oh king Rahugan; Etat—This knowledge; Tapasa—Through rigorous penance; Na Yati—Does not come; Na—Not; Cha—Also; Ijyaya—\nFrom great organization for the worship of Shrivigraha; Nirvapaat—Or from fulfilling all worldly duties and taking Sanyas; Grihath—From an ideal householder's life; Va—Or; Na—Neither; Chhandasam—By observing celibacy or by studying the Vedas; Na—Not;\nEv—Not; Jal-Agni-Suryaih—By staying in water, hot sun, or burning fire and performing rigorous penance; Vina—Without; Mahat—Of the supreme devotees; Pada-Rajah—In the dust of the feet; Abhisyam—Body washing, bathing..\n\nOh, King Rahugan, to smear the entire body with the dust of the lotus feet of the great men,\nthere can be no realization of the ultimate truth.\nTo observe celibacy, to follow the rules of household life,\nto renounce the household in the form of Vanaprastha or to take Sanyas, or to enter the water and perform severe penance, or to remain surrounded by fire in summer and lie in the scorching sun,\nsuch difficult penances cannot lead to the realization of the ultimate truth. There are many other means to know the ultimate truth, but the ultimate truth is attained only by one who receives the grace of a great devotee. Meaning: Only a pure devotee can bestow a true knowledge of Divine Bliss. Rare and uncommon among the Vedas, the devotion to the self. Only by following the commands of the Vedas, self-realization cannot be achieved.\nCan be achieved. The seeker of the pure devotee of the Lord has to reach the Lord—the one devoid of any other desire, devoid of any other’s knowledge or action. Through the grace of such a devotee, the supreme truth, Krishna, and one's relationship with him can be understood. A materialistic person sometimes thinks that by doing good deeds and staying at home, the ultimate\ntruth can be understood.\nThis verse is rejected. Neither can anyone know the ultimate truth by following the rules of celibacy. A human being only serves a pure devotee.\nIt happens. This will help you understand the absolute truth."
